---
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
import { getOptimizedAssetUrl } from "../../lib/assets";

const title = "Mihawk Is Banned: How Will the Meta Evolve?";
const description =
	"Dracule Mihawk is banned effective October 12, 2026. Discover how this major change could reshape the OP17 One Piece Card Game meta.";

const leaderArt = {
	xebec: "/assets/guides/Rocks.DXebec op17.png",
	elbafLuffy: "/assets/guides/Luffy OP17.png",
	sabo: "/assets/guides/Sabo OP13-004.png",
	kaido: "/assets/guides/Kaido op17.png",
	luffyAce: "/assets/guides/Luffy-st30.png",
	hancock: "/assets/guides/boa-hancock-op14.png",
	pudding: "/assets/guides/Pudding op08.png",
	robin: "/assets/guides/Nico-Robin-op09.png",
	ace: "/assets/guides/Ace-op16.png",
	shanks: "/assets/guides/Shanks op17.png",
	nami: "/assets/guides/nami-op11.png",
	teach: "/assets/guides/Teach-op16.png",
};
---

<MainLayout title={title} description={description}>
	<section class="hero meta-article-hero">
		<div class="container article-hero">
			<div class="article-hero-copy">
				<span class="hero-badge">Meta Analysis</span>
				<h1>Mihawk Is Banned: How Will the Meta Evolve?</h1>
				<p class="article-lead">
					The new One Piece Card Game Ban List, effective October 12, 2026, bans Dracule Mihawk, one of the Leaders
					that the current meta had been built around.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="article-section">
		<div class="container article-content">
			<div class="article-opening">
				<div class="article-opening-copy">
					<p>
						Mihawk was extremely well represented in tournaments. To compete in the format, other decks needed an answer
						for him. The meta therefore revolved around Mihawk, his counters, and sometimes even the decks that could beat
						those counters.
					</p>
					<p>
						His departure will necessarily change the balance of the format. Some decks lose their main bad matchup, while
						others instead lose one of the matchups that justified their place in the meta.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="banlist-hero-icon" aria-label="Dracule Mihawk banned leader">
					<img src={getOptimizedAssetUrl("/assets/guides/dracule-mihawk-op14.png")} alt="Dracule Mihawk" loading="eager" />
				</div>
			</div>

			<h2>The Biggest Winners: Xebec and Elbaf Luffy</h2>

			<article class="leader-block">
				<div class="leader-heading">
					<div class="leader-icon"><img src={getOptimizedAssetUrl(leaderArt.xebec)} alt="Rocks D. Xebec" loading="lazy" /></div>
					<h3>Rocks D. Xebec</h3>
				</div>
				<p>Rocks D. Xebec is the biggest winner from this Ban List.</p>
				<p>
					Mihawk was his biggest counter. Outside of that matchup, Xebec mainly has a negative matchup against Elbaf
					Luffy, but performs very well against the rest of the current meta.
				</p>
				<p>
					However, it is important to consider that decks have so far been primarily built to face Mihawk, not necessarily
					Xebec. If a large portion of Mihawk&apos;s representation shifts to Xebec, deck lists will logically start to evolve
					to face him more effectively.
				</p>
				<p>
					Xebec should therefore be extremely common at the start of the new format, but it remains to be seen how the
					meta adapts afterward.
				</p>
				<p class="leader-guide-link">
					Want to explore the decklist and game plan? Read the
					<a href="/deck-guides/rocks-d-xebec-op17/">Rocks D. Xebec OP17 Deck Guide</a>.
				</p>
			</article>

			<article class="leader-block">
				<div class="leader-heading">
					<div class="leader-icon leader-icon-elbaf-luffy"><img src={getOptimizedAssetUrl(leaderArt.elbafLuffy)} alt="Elbaf Luffy" loading="lazy" /></div>
					<h3>Elbaf Luffy</h3>
				</div>
				<p>Elbaf Luffy also benefits greatly from Mihawk&apos;s departure, as Mihawk was one of his very bad matchups.</p>
				<p>However, it still has other difficult matchups, especially Sabo and Luffy/Ace.</p>
				<p>
					Its main advantage in the future meta is its matchup against Xebec. If Xebec becomes as common as expected,
					Elbaf Luffy will have an important reason to remain at the top of the format.
				</p>
				<p>Xebec and Elbaf Luffy are therefore the two decks that benefit most directly from Mihawk&apos;s ban.</p>
				<p class="leader-guide-link">
					Want to explore the decklist and game plan? Read the
					<a href="/deck-guides/monkey-d-luffy-op17/">Monkey D. Luffy OP17 Deck Guide</a>.
				</p>
			</article>

			<h2>Decks That Should Remain Competitive</h2>

			<article class="leader-block">
				<div class="leader-heading">
					<div class="leader-icon"><img src={getOptimizedAssetUrl(leaderArt.sabo)} alt="Sabo OP13" loading="lazy" /></div>
					<h3>Sabo OP13</h3>
				</div>
				<p>Sabo does not benefit from the change in any particular way.</p>
				<p>
					Its matchup against Mihawk was even. It also performed well against Robin, whose popularity could decline after
					the ban.
				</p>
				<p>
					The deck can still hold a good place in the meta, but Mihawk&apos;s departure is not a direct advantage for it.
				</p>
				<p class="leader-guide-link">
					Want to explore the decklist and game plan? Read the
					<a href="/deck-guides/sabo-op13-format-op17/">Sabo OP13 Deck Guide</a>.
				</p>
			</article>

			<article class="leader-block">
				<div class="leader-heading">
					<div class="leader-icon"><img src={getOptimizedAssetUrl(leaderArt.kaido)} alt="Kaido OP17" loading="lazy" /></div>
					<h3>Kaido OP17</h3>
				</div>
				<p>Kaido benefits more from the ban.</p>
				<p>
					Mihawk could manage its large Characters with the Shanks + Decken combo, which was a real problem for the deck.
				</p>
				<p>
					Kaido still has difficult matchups against Luffy/Ace and Elbaf Luffy. Its other issue remains consistency: when
					it does not find its curve, its games become much more difficult.
				</p>
				<p>It therefore has more room in the new format, but its own consistency issues remain.</p>
				<p class="leader-guide-link">
					Want to explore the decklist and game plan? Read the
					<a href="/deck-guides/kaido-op17/">Kaido OP17 Deck Guide</a>.
				</p>
			</article>

			<article class="leader-block">
				<div class="leader-heading">
					<div class="leader-icon leader-icon-luffy-ace"><img src={getOptimizedAssetUrl(leaderArt.luffyAce)} alt="Luffy/Ace ST30" loading="lazy" /></div>
					<h3>Luffy/Ace ST30</h3>
				</div>
				<p>
					Luffy/Ace also benefits from Mihawk&apos;s departure. However, Robin&apos;s likely decline in popularity is not good news,
					since Luffy/Ace has a favorable matchup against her. It could therefore lose an opponent it was happy to face.
				</p>
				<p>
					The deck is well positioned against Kaido and Elbaf Luffy, but has more difficulty against Xebec.
				</p>
				<p>Its place will therefore depend heavily on Xebec&apos;s representation in the new meta.</p>
			</article>

			<article class="leader-block">
				<div class="leader-heading">
					<div class="leader-icon leader-icon-hancock"><img src={getOptimizedAssetUrl(leaderArt.hancock)} alt="Boa Hancock OP14" loading="lazy" /></div>
					<h3>Boa Hancock OP14</h3>
				</div>
				<p>Boa Hancock also gains from Mihawk&apos;s ban, especially if Robin becomes less popular.</p>
				<p>
					It remains slightly behind against Xebec and Kaido, although those matchups are not impossible to play. It could
					therefore be one of the more interesting yellow decks in the new format.
				</p>
				<p class="leader-guide-link">
					Want to explore the decklist and game plan? Read the
					<a href="/deck-guides/boa-hancock-op14-format-op17/">Boa Hancock OP14 Deck Guide</a>.
				</p>
			</article>

			<article class="leader-block">
				<div class="leader-heading">
					<div class="leader-icon"><img src={getOptimizedAssetUrl(leaderArt.pudding)} alt="Pudding OP08" loading="lazy" /></div>
					<h3>Pudding</h3>
				</div>
				<p>Pudding is another deck to watch.</p>
				<p>
					The Big Mom package fits naturally into the deck, thanks in part to the purple cards that let you play Katakuri earlier,
					as well as the 8-cost Katakuri, which can return your key characters to Active—particularly the 4,000-power Big Mom character package
					that gains +4,000 power during your turn thanks to the 10-cost Linlin (OP17).
				</p>
				<p>
					With Mihawk&apos;s ban and Robin&apos;s possible decline, Pudding could become more common. It is a deck players will need
					to be ready for if it starts gaining popularity.
				</p>
			</article>

			<h2>Decks That Lose the Most From the Ban</h2>

			<article class="leader-block">
				<div class="leader-heading">
					<div class="leader-icon leader-icon-robin"><img src={getOptimizedAssetUrl(leaderArt.robin)} alt="Robin OP09" loading="lazy" /></div>
					<h3>Robin OP09</h3>
				</div>
				<p>Robin is directly hurt by Mihawk&apos;s disappearance.</p>
				<p>
					A major part of its appeal came from its matchup against Mihawk. However, it has difficulties against several
					other important decks: Sabo, Luffy/Ace, Xebec, Kaido, and Elbaf Luffy.
				</p>
				<p>
					If those decks become more common while Mihawk disappears completely, Robin&apos;s current position becomes much more
					difficult.
				</p>
				<p>
					Its future deck lists may of course evolve, but with its current build, it appears to lose a great deal from this
					Ban List.
				</p>
				<p class="leader-guide-link">
					Want to explore the decklist and game plan? Read the
					<a href="/deck-guides/nico-robin-op09/">Nico Robin OP09 Deck Guide</a>.
				</p>
			</article>

			<article class="leader-block">
				<div class="leader-heading">
					<div class="leader-icon leader-icon-ace"><img src={getOptimizedAssetUrl(leaderArt.ace)} alt="Ace OP16" loading="lazy" /></div>
					<h3>Ace OP16</h3>
				</div>
				<p>Ace finds itself in a fairly similar situation.</p>
				<p>
					One of its main advantages was its ability to beat Mihawk. On the other hand, it loses to Luffy/Ace, Xebec, and
					Kaido, while having a good matchup against Elbaf Luffy.
				</p>
				<p>
					Mihawk&apos;s disappearance therefore removes one of its best matchups without removing enough of its current
					difficulties.
				</p>
				<p class="leader-guide-link">
					Want to explore the decklist and game plan? Read the
					<a href="/deck-guides/portags-d-ace-op16-format-op17/">Ace OP16 Deck Guide</a>.
				</p>
			</article>

			<h2>Decks to Watch</h2>

			<article class="leader-block">
				<div class="leader-heading">
					<div class="leader-icon"><img src={getOptimizedAssetUrl(leaderArt.shanks)} alt="Shanks OP17" loading="lazy" /></div>
					<h3>Shanks OP17</h3>
				</div>
				<p>Green Shanks had been overshadowed by Mihawk among green decks and could now find more room in the format.</p>
				<p>
					It still has certain limitations. It can still use Shanks + Decken, but does not have the full combo used by
					Mihawk. It can therefore manage a large Character, such as Kaido, without necessarily being able to manage the
					rest of the board behind it.
				</p>
				<p>Its potential will depend in particular on the new deck lists that emerge after the ban.</p>
				<p class="leader-guide-link">
					Want to explore the decklist and game plan? Read the
					<a href="/deck-guides/shanks-op17/">Shanks OP17 Deck Guide</a>.
				</p>
			</article>

			<article class="leader-block">
				<div class="leader-heading">
					<div class="leader-icon"><img src={getOptimizedAssetUrl(leaderArt.nami)} alt="Nami OP11" loading="lazy" /></div>
					<h3>Nami OP11</h3>
				</div>
				<p>Blue/Yellow Nami is also being discussed as a deck that could return after Mihawk&apos;s disappearance.</p>
				<p>
					In its current state, it still appears to have difficulties against the main expected decks. But its deck lists
					can evolve if strong players start working on the deck more.
				</p>
				<p>It therefore remains a possibility to watch rather than a deck already established at the top of the format.</p>
			</article>

			<article class="leader-block">
				<div class="leader-heading">
					<div class="leader-icon"><img src={getOptimizedAssetUrl(leaderArt.teach)} alt="Teach" loading="lazy" /></div>
					<h3>Teach and the Other Outsiders</h3>
				</div>
				<p>Other decks remain strong enough to reappear depending on how the format evolves.</p>
				<p>
					They may have several difficult matchups, especially against Xebec, but can become interesting again if certain
					decks start to dominate the meta.
				</p>
				<p>
					Teach is one of the decks capable of returning and putting up good results from time to time, even if it does
					not appear set to dominate the format immediately.
				</p>
			</article>

			<div class="meta-outlook">
				<div class="meta-outlook-heading">
					<span class="meta-outlook-label">Post-Ban Outlook</span>
					<h2>What Could the Meta Look Like After October 12?</h2>
				</div>
				<div class="meta-outlook-body">
					<p>
				With Mihawk&apos;s ban, Xebec should take a very important place in the new meta. It benefits directly from the
				disappearance of one of its main counters and currently has good matchups against a large part of the format.
					</p>
					<p>
				Elbaf Luffy should also remain very common. The deck currently has a stable version, benefits from Mihawk&apos;s
				departure, and most importantly has a good matchup against Xebec.
					</p>
					<p>
				Kaido also gains from this Ban List. Mihawk&apos;s disappearance allows it to play its large Characters more easily
				without having to deal with the Shanks + Decken combo as regularly. Its main problem remains consistency: when it
				does not find its curve, its games become much more difficult.
					</p>
					<p>
				Behind them, several decks remain capable of achieving very good results, while others will depend more on how
				builds evolve and which decks are actually the most represented after October 12.
					</p>
					<p class="meta-outlook-closing">
						Decklists will also start evolving to answer Xebec more effectively if it takes the place left by Mihawk. The
				meta of the first few weeks could therefore still change a great deal as decks adapt to the new format.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</MainLayout>

<style>
	.meta-article-hero {
		padding-bottom: 36px;
	}

	.article-hero {
		max-width: 920px;
	}

	.article-lead {
		max-width: 780px;
		font-size: 1.12rem;
		font-weight: 600;
		color: var(--text-100);
	}

	.article-opening {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 156px;
		gap: 2rem;
		align-items: center;
	}

	.article-opening-copy {
		display: grid;
		gap: 1.2rem;
	}

	.article-opening-copy > p {
		margin: 0;
	}

	.banlist-hero-icon {
		position: relative;
		width: 156px;
		aspect-ratio: 1;
		margin: 0 0 0 auto;
		overflow: hidden;
		border: 1px solid rgba(255, 83, 83, 0.52);
		border-radius: 22px;
		background: rgba(8, 11, 19, 0.95);
		box-shadow: 0 14px 30px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.32);
	}

	.banlist-hero-icon img {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		height: 100%;
		object-fit: cover;
		object-position: center top;
	}

	.banlist-hero-icon::before,
	.banlist-hero-icon::after {
		content: "";
		position: absolute;
		z-index: 1;
		pointer-events: none;
	}

	.banlist-hero-icon::before {
		inset: 0;
		background:
			radial-gradient(circle at center, rgba(255, 48, 48, 0.18), rgba(96, 0, 0, 0.12) 64%, transparent 75%),
			linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(20, 0, 0, 0.1), rgba(110, 0, 0, 0.32));
		box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(255, 83, 83, 0.42);
	}

	.banlist-hero-icon::after {
		inset: -1px;
		background:
			linear-gradient(45deg, transparent 47%, rgba(45, 0, 0, 0.88) 47.3% 48%, #ff3838 48.3% 50%, rgba(255, 122, 122, 0.96) 50.3% 50.7%, rgba(45, 0, 0, 0.88) 51% 51.7%, transparent 52%),
			linear-gradient(-45deg, transparent 47%, rgba(45, 0, 0, 0.88) 47.3% 48%, #ff3838 48.3% 50%, rgba(255, 122, 122, 0.96) 50.3% 50.7%, rgba(45, 0, 0, 0.88) 51% 51.7%, transparent 52%);
		filter: drop-shadow(0 1px 3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.7));
	}

	.article-section {
		padding: 0 0 64px;
	}

	.article-content {
		max-width: 920px;
	}

	.article-content > p,
	.leader-block > p {
		max-width: 780px;
		color: var(--text-200);
		line-height: 1.75;
	}

	.article-content > h2 {
		margin: 3.3rem 0 1.4rem;
		padding-top: 0.1rem;
		font-size: clamp(1.65rem, 3vw, 2.25rem);
	}

	.leader-block {
		margin: 2.2rem 0 3rem;
		padding: 1.25rem;
		background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(255, 138, 42, 0.16), rgba(44, 117, 255, 0.14));
		border: 1px solid rgba(255, 138, 42, 0.28);
		border-radius: 22px;
		box-shadow: 0 16px 40px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.28), 0 0 24px rgba(255, 138, 42, 0.08);
	}

	.leader-block > p:last-child {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	.leader-guide-link {
		font-weight: 700;
	}

	.leader-guide-link a {
		color: #ff8b3d;
		text-decoration: none;
	}

	.leader-guide-link a:hover {
		color: #ffb06f;
		text-decoration: underline;
	}

	.meta-outlook {
		margin-top: 3.4rem;
		padding: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 2rem);
		background:
			radial-gradient(circle at 8% 0%, rgba(255, 138, 42, 0.2), transparent 38%),
			linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(255, 138, 42, 0.13), rgba(18, 25, 44, 0.92) 58%, rgba(10, 14, 24, 0.96));
		border: 1px solid rgba(255, 138, 42, 0.34);
		border-radius: 26px;
		box-shadow: 0 18px 44px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3), 0 0 28px rgba(255, 138, 42, 0.08);
	}

	.meta-outlook-heading {
		padding-bottom: 1.15rem;
		border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 138, 42, 0.24);
	}

	.meta-outlook-label {
		display: inline-flex;
		padding: 0.38rem 0.7rem;
		border-radius: 999px;
		background: rgba(255, 123, 47, 0.2);
		color: #ff9a52;
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		font-weight: 800;
		letter-spacing: 0.08em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
	}

	.meta-outlook-heading h2 {
		margin: 0.85rem 0 0;
		font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 3vw, 2.35rem);
	}

	.meta-outlook-body {
		display: grid;
		gap: 1rem;
		padding-top: 1.15rem;
	}

	.meta-outlook-body p {
		margin: 0;
		max-width: none;
		color: var(--text-100);
		line-height: 1.78;
	}

	.meta-outlook-closing {
		padding-top: 1.1rem;
		border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 138, 42, 0.24);
		font-weight: 650;
	}

	.leader-heading {
		display: grid;
		justify-items: center;
		gap: 0.8rem;
		margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
		text-align: center;
	}

	.leader-heading h3 {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 2.4vw, 1.75rem);
	}

	.leader-icon {
		width: 132px;
		aspect-ratio: 1;
		overflow: hidden;
		border: 1px solid rgba(255, 138, 42, 0.28);
		border-radius: 20px;
		background: rgba(8, 11, 19, 0.95);
		box-shadow: 0 12px 28px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.28);
	}

	.leader-icon img {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		height: 100%;
		object-fit: cover;
		object-position: center top;
	}

	@media (min-width: 721px) {
		.leader-block {
			position: relative;
			min-height: 190px;
			padding: 4.4rem 1.25rem 1.25rem 174px;
		}

		.leader-heading {
			position: absolute;
			top: 1.25rem;
			left: 1.25rem;
			display: block;
			width: 132px;
			margin: 0;
		}

		.leader-heading h3 {
			position: absolute;
			top: 1.15rem;
			left: calc(100% + 1.25rem);
			width: 520px;
			text-align: left;
		}

		.leader-block:nth-of-type(even) {
			padding-right: 174px;
			padding-left: 1.25rem;
		}

		.leader-block:nth-of-type(even) .leader-heading {
			left: auto;
			right: 1.25rem;
		}

		.leader-block:nth-of-type(even) .leader-heading h3 {
			left: auto;
			right: calc(100% + 1.25rem);
			text-align: right;
		}
	}

	@media (max-width: 720px) {
		.meta-article-hero {
			padding-bottom: 28px;
		}

		.article-section {
			padding-bottom: 48px;
		}

		.article-hero {
			display: block;
		}

		.article-opening {
			display: flex;
			flex-direction: column;
			gap: 1.2rem;
		}

		.article-opening-copy {
			order: 2;
		}

		.article-lead {
			font-size: 1rem;
		}

		.banlist-hero-icon {
			width: 100%;
			aspect-ratio: 3.25 / 1;
			border-radius: 18px;
			margin: 0 auto;
			order: 1;
		}

		.banlist-hero-icon img {
			object-position: center 68%;
		}

		.article-content > h2 {
			margin-top: 2.65rem;
		}

		.leader-block {
			margin: 1.6rem 0 2.25rem;
			padding: 1rem;
			border-radius: 18px;
		}

		.leader-heading {
			gap: 0.7rem;
		}

		.leader-icon {
			width: 100%;
			aspect-ratio: 3.25 / 1;
			border-radius: 16px;
		}

		.leader-icon img {
			object-position: center 28%;
		}

		.leader-icon-robin img {
			object-position: center top;
		}

		.leader-icon-elbaf-luffy img {
			object-position: center 70%;
		}

		.leader-icon-luffy-ace img {
			object-position: center 18%;
		}

		.leader-icon-hancock img {
			object-position: center 18%;
